  Yeah, I had the same thought! Imagine lojban as an Idioglossia.

   As far as the sign language, I agree with you, Robin.  The modalities
are completely different.  People who are unfamiliar with the concept think
that ASL is somehow related to English.  It isn't, in the slightest (my
wife is fluent in ASL, and I'm... well, sorta fluent would be kind).  It
just happens to be spoken by people in the same country.  And SEE is just
cruel, and slows the deaf down.  So the concept of some sort of "lojbanic
sign language" is just silly Now, on the other hand, using signs with kids
IS a good idea, for the reasons Sebastian mentioned.
                       --gejyspa

